; ANSWER_DIVERGES_BY_VANTAGE_POINT — the honest side.
;
; A name whose SPF record differs depending on which authoritative server you
; ask. This side authorises the platform; zones/divergent/split.test.zone does
; not. Both servers are listed in the delegation, so a resolver may legitimately
; pick either and two vantage points reach different conclusions about the same
; domain — which is exactly what the consensus logic consumes.
;
; This is the real shape of mid-propagation: nobody is lying, one server simply
; has not caught up. It is also the shape of the "it works for me" support
; ticket, and the reason a single disagreeing vantage point must never be able
; to produce `failed` on its own.
;
; `drift.test` diverges too, but only in its SOA serial, so every evaluator
; reaches the same verdict from both servers and the consensus logic sees
; nothing. This zone diverges in a record an evaluator actually reads.
;
; Keep the NS RRset in step with zones/divergent/split.test.zone. If the two
; disagree about the delegation itself this stops testing divergent answers and
; starts testing a broken delegation.
$ORIGIN split.test.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 300 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.
@       IN NS   ns-divergent.test.

; Authorises the platform. The divergent side does not.
@                   IN TXT  "v=spf1 include:one.spf.test -all"

; The selector the platform issued, present on this side only.
;
; The SPF divergence above cannot move a domain's state, and that is not a
; defect in it: evaluating `include:one.spf.test` needs zones dns-divergent does
; not serve, so from that vantage point the answer is SPF_TEMPORARY_FAILURE —
; `indeterminate`, which by design changes nothing. Correct for SPF, useless for
; anything that has to observe a transition.
;
; DKIM is one lookup inside this zone, so dns-divergent answers NODATA on its
; own authority and the verdict is a clean `fail`. That is what lets the
; end-to-end spec drive verified -> degraded -> failed -> recovered by moving
; between vantage points, with no zone edited while a test is running.
;
; The key is byte-identical to customer.test's, so one expectation value serves
; both domains. Keep it that way, or `cli.e2e.spec.ts` needs two.
pg1._domainkey         IN TXT ( "v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p=MIIBIjANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAAOCAQ8AMIIBCgKCAQEApbBuIv1NwQ/rmgGPN8OufvLBfd2asvyk4ajVkiW2CsC12MohickhGufJPsGNyO/ZXD4b/HnClukz07BZwpJe80wz0w/AfhJCqM8F3v/aVHF7wWnd9wBBPBroTL7kNx5u39NnBZZj8SYJF7zNaQ3rud4ekF+GAyTovT7MUfXHQBgEZn0n5Y4dN7b7VEMi4/97T"
                                "DCNDJucFywdDmbJ9r6LaCu+l+gWfZGl4rDimTJCw3oCQIpCOGlNrWPwxRLuB0sLR2gR1GT9EqBg3yGforXasq2wqBuZlpI1YXmdldEZh3VRIyft4TeVTHRJAf7/TKuAINb8+LOoXHj5hFYl+C4zUQIDAQAB" )
